"""
Scientific HRV analysis pipeline (ECG → R-peaks → RR → time/frequency HRV).
Version 2.5 (4 Oct 2026) — refactor of GPR_Improved.py.
    2.1  waterfall plot removed from the dashboard.
    2.2  ectopic beats flag the right intervals [D13]; dashboard beat markers
         fixed [D14]; edge beats of a trial judged with neighbours [D15].
    2.3  artefact-detector thresholds capped at 20 % of the local RR, so
         frequent ectopy no longer hides itself [D16]; metrics that cannot be
         computed are left empty instead of 0 [D17].
    2.4  both intervals around every premature beat are corrected, so the
         premature beat itself is re-timed [D18]; intervals shorter than 60 %
         of the local rhythm (beats detected on noise) are always flagged
         [D19]; trials named for the other medication state are skipped [D20].
    2.5  a sustained short–long alternation (a bigeminal rhythm, as in P-014)
         is flagged and excluded as a whole [D21]; RMSSD no longer bridges an
         excluded stretch [D22]; the bad beats are split into dropped /
         corrected / kept in the workbook, console and dashboard [D23];
         slightly early beats the detector cannot see are counted [D24];
         a state can list several EDF file patterns, so P-001's and P-011's
         ON 5-min sitting (recorded in separate rest files) is analysed
         [D25]; a hole in the ECG recording inside a trial is excluded
         instead of bridged [D26]; a trial can be read from another ECG lead
         (P-015 OFF 5-min sitting from ECG 2, as its run sheet says) [D27].

HOW TO RUN
    BATCH_MODE = True   every participant/state in PARTICIPANTS → one workbook each
    BATCH_MODE = False  PARTICIPANT/STATE only, then the interactive dashboard
    DRY_RUN    = True   (default) nothing is written. Batch mode runs a fast
                        PREFLIGHT of every registry entry (EDF files found, ECG
                        channel present, sync log parsed, trial count, workbook to
                        be written) that also lists trials logged as under 5 s
                        (placeholders), trials outside the EDF files and trials
                        with part of the window unrecorded [D25, D26]. Single mode
                        processes and opens the dashboard but skips the workbook.
    DRY_RUN    = False  process and write HRV_Analysis_<PID>_<STATE>.xlsx into
                        OUTPUT_DIR. File names are unchanged, so the R script only
                        needs HRV_DIR pointed at OUTPUT_DIR. The September
                        Preliminary_HRV_Values workbooks are not touched.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
METHODS STATEMENT
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Spectral estimation — Lomb–Scargle is PRIMARY, Welch is SECONDARY.
    RR series are intrinsically unevenly sampled, and become more uneven after
    ectopic/missed-beat removal. The Lomb–Scargle periodogram estimates the
    spectrum directly from the irregular (t, RR) samples, so it neither
    requires nor distorts the data through resampling. The Welch path must
    first cubic-spline-interpolate the RR series onto a 4 Hz grid; that
    interpolation acts as a low-pass filter and injects autocorrelation across
    gaps, biasing HF power and LF/HF — exactly where artifact gaps live. We
    therefore report Lomb–Scargle as the primary spectral estimate and retain
    Welch only as a secondary cross-check for comparability with legacy tools
    (the dashboard defaults to Lomb–Scargle for this reason). Both paths use
    identical band edges (VLF 0.003–0.04, LF 0.04–0.15, HF 0.15–0.40 Hz; Task
    Force 1996) so the two estimates are directly comparable. Every spectrum is
    computed on REAL beat times: for the corrected series these are the
    reconstructed R-peak times, and unrecoverable gaps remain gaps in time.

RR reconstruction — physiology-driven beat count, GP-shaped values, exact timing.
    Each maximal run of L flagged intervals spanning `gap` samples is assigned
    k = round(gap / RR_local) expected physiologic intervals (RR_local = mean
    of the clean intervals bracketing the run). k > L ⇒ missed beats (k − L
    beats inserted); k < L ⇒ extra beats (merged to k intervals); k = L ⇒ the
    count is right but the values are outliers (replaced). Runs that would need
    more than MAX_MISSED_BEATS insertions, insertions across more than
    MAX_GAP_RECONSTRUCT_SEC, or replacement of more than MAX_REPLACE_RUN_SEC
    are kept intact and FLAGGED as unrecoverable, so downstream analyses
    exclude them rather than fabricating beats.
    Values of reconstructed intervals come from Gaussian-process regression
    (GPR) fitted to the clean intervals of the trial, with the input x = beat
    order (Mojtahed et al. 2026, Sec. 3.3) and a Rational-Quadratic + white-
    noise covariance. The reconstructed BEATS are placed so that each run tiles
    its observed gap exactly, giving one monotone timeline for spectral
    analysis. For missed-beat and extra-beat runs — detection errors, which
    leave the heart's true beat times unchanged — the reconstructed values are
    those beat spacings and therefore sum to the gap, unless the gap does not
    hold a whole number of beats at the local rhythm (beat count ambiguous;
    values then stay at the local rhythm). For replaced runs — rhythm events
    such as premature beats, which can reset the sinus node — the values are
    the GP estimate of the local sinus RR and are not forced to sum to the gap
    (see [D2]). A single flagged interval whose count is already right
    (L = k = 1) is bounded by two real beats; only its value is substituted.
    See perform_artifact_correction().

Validation benchmark — Lipponen–Tarvainen (2019) / Kubios, with acceptance
    criteria. The custom correction is cross-checked against the published
    Kubios artifact-correction method (NeuroKit2 signal_fixpeaks, method=
    "kubios"). Per-trial agreement is computed for HR, SDNN, RMSSD and LF/HF
    and judged PASS/WARN/FAIL against the BENCHMARK_TOLERANCES below. These
    tolerances are reproducibility-based defaults — adjust them to your study's
    requirements before drawing conclusions. See benchmark_against_kubios().
    NOTE: the reference runs NeuroKit2 with the PUBLISHED detector settings,
    whereas this pipeline uses KUBIOS_ALPHA = 8.0, KUBIOS_MRRS_THRESH = 4.5, a
    35-ms threshold floor, (2.3) a threshold ceiling and (2.4) the rules
    [D18]/[D19], so some disagreement is by design. In trials with frequent
    ectopy the reference cannot see the ectopic beats [D16], so FAIL verdicts
    there are expected.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DECISIONS — VERSION 2 (numbered for the methods section / audit trail)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[D1]  Kernel. RationalQuadratic + WhiteKernel replaces RBF + WhiteKernel,
      following Mojtahed et al. (2026; Table 9: RBF 17.9 % vs RQ 7.37 %
      overall HRV deviation; Sec. 5.2: RBF over-smooths). On our data the
      practical effect is small: the previous RBF fit was usually well-behaved
      (median length scale 5.4 beats on 80 real trials; it collapsed to its
      1e-5 bound only on the synthetic test recording), and RQ vs RBF moves
      the corrected values little (VALIDATION). GPR_KERNEL = 'rbf' reproduces
      the previous kernel for sensitivity analyses.
[D2]  One timeline; values sum to the gap only where physiology requires it.
      Previously the analysed timeline was cumsum(values) [D6] while the
      exported one placed inserted beats by equal division and left 'replaced'
      runs at the original (e.g. ectopic) beat positions, so values and beat
      times disagreed by up to 650 ms in the September workbooks. Now the
      reconstructed beats always tile the observed gap (GP shape + an equal
      share of the residual) and that one timeline is used everywhere.
      Values: for missed-beat ('inserted') and extra-beat ('merged') runs —
      detection errors, true beat times unaffected — values = beat spacings,
      so they sum to the gap (the fix proposed in August), provided the gap
      holds k beats at the local rhythm (|gap/k − rhythm| ≤ 10 %,
      RR_SUM_MAX_OFFSET; rhythm = mean GP estimate over the run). Genuine
      (simulated) detection errors pass 97.6 % of the time (offset median
      0.5 %), but 35 % of the September missed/extra-beat runs do not (median
      5.8 %): their beat count is ambiguous — e.g. P-001 ON 30s_T3, where an
      undetected premature beat beside the run makes RR_local 513 ms against a
      ~625-ms rhythm, so k = 3 would split 1.43 s into three 477-ms
      intervals. Such runs keep values at the local rhythm and
      Reconstruction_QC labels them 'ambiguous' (Count_Fit_Offset_Pct).
      For 'replaced' runs — premature beats / timing noise — values stay at
      the GP estimate of the local rhythm. Forcing those to sum to the gap
      (the Kubios beat-level convention) was tested and rejected: after a
      sinus-resetting premature beat it inflated RMSSD by 54–62 % (VALIDATION),
      and such runs occur in our data (e.g. P-017 ON 5-min standing: premature
      beat + pause ~270 ms short of compensatory). Re-tested in 2.4 with the
      10 % guard applied to replaced runs as well: better after compensatory
      pauses, far worse after sinus-resetting ones (VALIDATION 15), so not
      adopted. RR_SUM_CONSTRAINT = 'all' (Kubios-like) or 'none' (never) are
      available for sensitivity analyses.
[D3]  One GP fit and ONE vectorised predict() per trial (previously one call
      per synthesised interval). The posterior SD is kept and reported per
      run in the Reconstruction_QC sheet.
[D4]  Reproducibility. The optimizer restarts are seeded (GPR_RANDOM_STATE).
      The previous code was not seeded: re-running it on identical input gave
      different corrected values (e.g. P-004 OFF_30s_T2 RMSSD 23.8–26.4 ms
      across reruns); 27 of the 985 September trials do not reproduce.
[D5]  One correction pass per trial. The correction used to run twice (once
      for the exported RR series, again inside calculate_hrv_metrics), so with
      [D4] the exported corrected series and the metrics could come from two
      different fits. Now both come from the same result.
[D6]  Corrected timeline. Corrected spectra used to be computed on
      cumsum(corrected RR) after dropping unrecoverable gaps, which splices the
      series shut across each gap and drifts wherever values ≠ beat spacing.
      Now the reconstructed R-peak times are used (gaps stay gaps), as the
      Lomb–Scargle rationale above requires. Time-domain metrics are unaffected
      by this item (they use values only).
[D7]  Duration_Sec (Data_Quality) was 0 for every trial (it was read from the
      Welch result, which never stores it). It is now the time from the first
      to the last detected R-peak in the trial window.
[D8]  GP input axis. Clean intervals are placed on an estimated beat-order axis
      (+1 per clean interval, +k per flagged run, including unrecoverable
      gaps), so intervals after a missed-beat run are not shifted in "beat
      number" relative to those before it.
[D9]  Unchanged: R-peak detection, morphology check, the artefact detector's
      beat classification (KUBIOS_ALPHA 8.0, KUBIOS_MRRS_THRESH 4.5, 35-ms
      floor — now the named constant KUBIOS_TH_FLOOR_MS), run classification
      (k, actions, limits), spectral estimators and the benchmark. Raw RR
      series and raw metrics are identical to GPR_Improved.py. Since 2.2 the
      INTERVALS flagged around ectopic beats [D13] and at trial edges [D15]
      differ, so flag counts and burden change for trials with such beats.
      Since 2.3 the detector's thresholds have a ceiling [D16], which changes
      the flags (and burden) in trials with frequent ectopy. Since 2.4 the
      interval after a premature beat [D18] and intervals shorter than 60 % of
      the local rhythm [D19] are flagged as well. R-peak detection itself is
      still unchanged: a QRS-enhancing (matched) filter was tested on the
      noisiest screenshot and cannot help there (VALIDATION 17). Since 2.5 a
      sustained alternation is flagged as a whole [D21].
[D10] Outputs. Sheets 1–7 and their columns are unchanged. Data_Quality gains
      four columns at the END (Intervals_Replaced / _Inserted / _Merged,
      Reconstructed_Percent). Two sheets are appended: Reconstruction_QC (one
      row per flagged run: L, k, action, gap/RR_local, Count_Fit_Offset_Pct,
      where the values came from (incl. 'ambiguous'), posterior SD, timing-
      vs morphology-flagged) and Run_Config (parameters + library versions).
      Workbooks go to OUTPUT_DIR; DRY_RUN guards every write. 2.5 appends nine
      Data_Quality columns and five Reconstruction_QC columns [D23, D24];
      earlier columns keep their place and meaning (RMSSD, SDSD and pNN50
      change only where a stretch is excluded [D22]).
[D11] Sensitivity options. RR_FILL_METHOD = 'equal' (no GP: equal beats and
      equal values within a run), GPR_KERNEL = 'rbf' (previous kernel) and
      RR_SUM_CONSTRAINT = 'all' / 'none'. Their effects on the September data
      are reported under VALIDATION.
[D12] Citation. sklearn computes the standard GP posterior mean
      K*(K + σ²I)⁻¹y (Rasmussen & Williams 2006, Eq. 2.23). Mojtahed et al.'s
      Eq. 14 prints the mean term as K*(K⁻¹ + σ⁻²I)y, inconsistent with its own
      covariance term — cite Rasmussen & Williams for the formula.
[D13] Ectopic beats flag the intervals around the ectopic beat (2.2). The
      Lipponen–Tarvainen detector reports an ectopic beat at the CENTRE of its
      dRR pattern — for a premature beat, the long interval after it — so the
      abnormal beat is the one before the reported index. The previous mapping
      flagged the pause and the NORMAL interval after it, left the premature
      beat's short interval in the 'clean' series, and could make the run look
      like a missed beat (e.g. P-001 OFF 5-min standing, 08:51:07.900: a
      premature beat was kept, a beat was fabricated and the real beat at
      08:51:09.017 was moved). Simulated premature beats: false flags 12 → 0
      per 12 events; NeuroKit's kubios correction (the benchmark) shows the
      same one-beat shift (it moves the post-pause beat too).
[D14] Dashboard (2.2). The 'Corrected Peak Alignments' panel indexed
      reconstructed beats with ORIGINAL beat numbers, so after any inserted or
      removed beat every marker was shifted and some real beats had none. Beats
      are now matched by time: green = kept, blue + = reconstructed position,
      red × = detected beat that was moved/removed. In 'Original Signal', red X
      marks only beats the correction moved or removed (a flagged run's two
      boundary beats are real and stay green). Beat times now come from the
      recording's own sample timestamps. Burden columns are unchanged in
      definition (they still count boundary beats).
[D15] Trial edges (2.2). Timing artefacts are detected on the padded beat
      series (PAD_SECONDS of ECG is already loaded either side) and cut back
      to the window, so the first and last beats are judged with neighbours on
      both sides. In the September 30-s trials, 29 of 226 had an unflagged
      first/last interval > 20 % off the trial median (12 > 30 %). On 1,103
      real 30-s windows cut from 5-min trials, edge flags missed vs a full-
      series reference fell from 84 to 15. More context helps further in
      steady data (30 s: windows with RMSSD > 5 % off the reference 62 → 10),
      but around the pre-walk trials it would include the walk, which could
      not be tested here — raise PAD_SECONDS only after checking.
[D16] Threshold ceiling (2.3). The detector's thresholds are α × a quartile
      deviation over 91 beats (Th1 of |dRR|, Th2 of the deviation from the
      local median). A quartile deviation tolerates up to a quarter of abnormal
      values, and every premature or extra beat produces two or three large
      dRR values, so from roughly one ectopic beat in ten the thresholds grow
      past the ectopic beats themselves and none of them is flagged. Example:
      P-009 ON 5-min sitting, three interpolated beats within ten beats (two
      short intervals summing to one 1.27-s interval) sat under a Th1 of
      1.6–2.1 s; only the one the morphology check caught was corrected. Th1
      and Th2 are now capped at KUBIOS_TH_CEIL_FRAC (0.20) × the local median
      RR, the long-standing 20 % rule for intervals that are not normal-to-
      normal (Kleiger et al. 1987, Am J Cardiol 59:256–262). Thresholds below
      the ceiling are unchanged: 810 of the 985 September trials keep exactly
      the same flags (VALIDATION 10). 0.20 rather than 0.25 or 0.30 because
      mildly premature beats (coupling 75–80 % of the RR) are missed above
      0.20 (VALIDATION 9). KUBIOS_TH_CEIL_FRAC = None restores 2.2's detector
      (with [D18]/[D19] switched off as well).
[D17] Empty instead of 0 (2.3). With fewer than 5 intervals left,
      calculate_time_domain_hrv() returned HR = SDNN = RMSSD = 0, which reads
      as a real value. Since [D16] this happens in real trials: P-016 has ectopy
      every third beat throughout (both states), so 33 of its trials are
      flagged end to end and excluded as unrecoverable. Such cells are now
      empty (NaN in Python, NA in R); NN_Count_* still gives the count.
[D18] Premature beats: both intervals (2.4). The interval that ends in a
      premature beat and the one that starts at it are both non-NN (Task
      Force 1996). The detector's 'ectopic' class needs short–long–normal: a
      compensatory pause after which the rhythm drops straight back. When the
      premature beat resets the sinus node (the next interval is one normal
      cycle), or the pause is followed by several lengthened sinus intervals
      (post-extrasystolic lengthening, frequent in P-010), only the short
      interval was flagged ('long/short'). That run is one interval with the
      right beat count (L = k = 1), so only its value was replaced: the
      premature beat stayed where it was (green on the dashboard) and the
      interval after it stayed in the NN series (screenshots: P-010 ON 5-min
      sitting 12:50:02.97, P-010 OFF 5-min sitting, P-025 ON 5-min standing
      09:49:05.20). Now, when a run of flagged intervals ends in an interval
      shorter than 90 % (PREMATURE_MIN_FRAC) of the rhythm just before the run
      (median of the last 5 NN intervals), the next interval is flagged too:
      the run grows by one and the premature beat is re-timed. If that
      interval is short as well (premature beats in a row), the next one is
      examined the same way (at most 3 per run). Not when the run has two or
      more intervals totalling a whole number of beats (±10 %,
      PREMATURE_GRID_TOL): the beat ending it is then back on the sinus grid
      (an extra beat: short intervals summing to one beat; a late beat: long
      + short). 10 % rather than 15 %: after P-009's interpolated beats the
      next sinus beat arrives 10–14 % of a cycle late (e.g. 706 + 460 ms at a
      1,038-ms rhythm), so the interval after it is short (921 ms) and not
      normal-to-normal either. The values filled in follow [D2] (local
      rhythm, not forced to sum to the gap). September cohort: premature
      beats corrected on one side only 90 → 1 (VALIDATION 16).
      PREMATURE_MIN_FRAC = None turns it off; with RR_REL_MIN_FRAC = None as
      well the detector is exactly 2.3's.
[D19] Implausibly short intervals (2.4). In noisy stretches the R-peak
      detector places beats on noise. A single spurious beat is caught by the
      'extra' class, but several within a few seconds pull the detector's
      11-beat local median down with them, so the short intervals no longer
      look short (P-021 ON 5-min standing, 11:32:45–50: local median 535 ms
      against a ~850-ms rhythm; a 371-ms interval stayed in the NN series).
      An interval shorter than 60 % of the local rhythm (RR_REL_MIN_FRAC) is
      now always flagged, like intervals outside MIN_RR_MS–MAX_RR_MS. Local
      rhythm = median of the unflagged intervals within ±15
      (RHYTHM_HALF_WINDOW), so ectopic or noise beats do not pull it down. A
      genuine premature beat that early is normally flagged by the detector
      already, so the rule adds flags only where its classes fail: 14
      intervals in 7 of the 985 September trials, and interpolated beats at
      every 3rd beat, which 2.3 mostly missed (VALIDATION 15, 16).
      RR_REL_MIN_FRAC = None turns it off.
[D20] Medication state (2.4). Trials whose sync-log name starts with the
      other state ('ON_…' while processing OFF, and vice versa) are skipped,
      with a note in the console; names without an ON/OFF prefix are kept.
      P-015's OFF EDF files also cover the ON session and its sync log lists
      both, so the 2.3 OFF workbook held 23 ON trials (49 instead of 26). The R
      script matches trials with the state prefix removed (trial_key), so they
      would have entered P-015's OFF burden (5-min standing: the mean of the
      OFF and ON trials, 5.5 and 28.9 %) and been counted twice in the Aim 4
      burden. The preflight's trial count applies the same filter.
[D21] Alternating rhythm (2.5). P-014 alternates short–long every beat for
      minutes: OFF 5-min sitting ~750 / ~910 ms around ~830 ms in 99 % of the
      trial, ON 5-min standing 65 %, ON 5-min walking 86 %, none in OFF
      standing or walking. Each beat looks normal — same QRS, and the P wave
      before the early beats has the same shape as before the late ones
      (VALIDATION 19) — so these are late atrial premature beats from near
      the sinus node (atrial bigeminy) or a sinus conduction pattern; a
      clinician has to read the strip. Either way it is not the autonomic
      variability HRV measures: it is not breathing (18 breaths/min at 72 bpm,
      4 beats per breath), and it dominates RMSSD (raw 154 ms in OFF sitting).
      In 2.4 the swing sat at the detector's 20 % ceiling, so the same rhythm
      was handled three ways in one trial: 12 % kept as normal-to-normal, 21 %
      replaced by smooth reconstructed beats, 67 % excluded as flagged
      stretches longer than 15 s (the beats the dashboard showed as removed).
      Now a stretch of at least 8 successive differences alternating in sign
      (9 intervals, ALTERNATION_MIN_RUN), every interval ≥ 5 % from the mean
      of its pair (ALTERNATION_MIN_AMP), whose short + long pairs span two
      beats of the rhythm around it (≥ 1.5, ALTERNATION_PAIR_MIN_BEATS; a pair
      holding one beat is an extra detection and is left to the extra-beat
      rules), is flagged as a whole ('alternating rhythm', the intervals at
      both ends included) and excluded, never reconstructed — the rhythm
      underneath is not observable there. ALTERNATION_HANDLING = 'keep'
      treats it as NN instead (a sensitivity run if a clinician reads P-014 as
      sinus rhythm; it keeps true bigeminy too), 'off' restores 2.4.
      Data_Quality reports Alternating_Rhythm_Percent under any setting. In
      the September data the rule also finds bigeminy runs that 2.4 flagged
      only partly (P-001 OFF, P-026 ON), noise already flagged (P-012 ON,
      P-021 ON walking), 10 % of P-023 OFF 5-min sitting (~830 / ~950 ms,
      unflagged in 2.4), 3 % of P-011 OFF 5-min sitting (inside an irregular
      stretch with premature beats; the trial is 71 % flagged anyway) and 2 %
      of P-003 OFF 5-min walking (543 / 605 ms — possibly steps pacing the
      heartbeat; walking trials only). Breathing
      does not trigger it unless a breath lasts about two beats with an RR
      swing of ≥ ±8 % (VALIDATION 20).
[D22] Successive differences (2.5). RMSSD, SDSD and pNN50 of the corrected
      series were computed after dropping the excluded stretches and closing
      the series up, so the interval before a stretch and the one after it
      counted as a successive difference (e.g. P-011 OFF 30s_T1: 586 → 1,059 ms
      across a 9-s excluded stretch, RMSSD 105.6 → 63.8 ms without it). They
      now use only intervals that are adjacent in the reconstruction; HR and
      SDNN are unchanged. With fewer than 4 such differences RMSSD/SDSD/pNN50
      are left empty [D17]. Trials without an excluded stretch are unchanged.
[D23] Where the bad beats went (2.5). Unique_Bad_Beats counts every beat that
      touches a flagged interval (the trial's first and last beats excepted)
      or has an abnormal shape, so it mixes beats that were dropped, moved and
      left alone. Data_Quality gains, at the end:
      Beats_Dropped = Dropped_Extra_Beats (extra beats removed) +
      Dropped_Excluded (beats inside stretches left out of the analysis, of
      which Excluded_Alternating for [D21]); Beats_Corrected (detected beats
      moved to the reconstructed position: premature beats, timing noise);
      Bad_Beats_Kept (flagged but left in place: the two real beats that
      bound each corrected stretch, and both beats of a single interval whose
      value was replaced); Beats_Inserted (missed beats added — never
      detected, so not part of Unique_Bad_Beats); Alternating_Rhythm_Percent;
      Unflagged_Early_Beats_Percent [D24]. In every trial Unique_Bad_Beats =
      Beats_Dropped + Beats_Corrected + Bad_Beats_Kept (VALIDATION 18, 20).
      Reconstruction_QC gets the same counts per run and Exclusion_Reason
      (alternating rhythm / flagged stretch longer than 15 s / missed beats
      over more than 5 s / more than 4 missed beats). The console line of
      each trial and the dashboard show the same split; on the dashboard,
      excluded beats are grey × ('Excluded (not analysed)', the reason on
      hover) and moved or removed beats stay red ×.
[D24] Unflagged early beats (2.5, quality check). An unflagged interval at
      least 10 % shorter than the local median of 9 intervals (EARLY_CHECK_
      FRAC), with both neighbours ≥ 95 % of it, ends in a slightly early beat
      followed straight away by the normal rhythm — a late premature beat.
      The detector resolves 20 % [D16], so such beats stay in the NN series.
      Data_Quality reports Unflagged_Early_Beats_Percent; the console and the
      dashboard warn at ≥ 5 % (EARLY_CHECK_WARN_PCT). In the September data it
      is 0 in 90 % of trials (95th percentile 0.2 %); P-014 OFF 5-min
      standing (18.9 %: looks normal, 3.7 % flagged) and P-015 (5–15 % in 18
      of its 49 trials) stand out. Breathing with large, fast swings (≥ ±8 % at
      ≤ 5 beats per breath) also produces hits, so by default nothing is
      flagged (EARLY_BEAT_HANDLING = 'report'); 'flag' treats them as
      premature beats (in 'kubios' mode [D18] usually adds the interval after
      them, so the beat is re-timed) — a decision for the study, ideally
      after a clinician has read P-014's and P-015's strips (VALIDATION 22).
[D25] Several file patterns per state (2.5). P-001's and P-011's ON 5-min
      sitting was recorded during the IART task, in separate files
      (PD_P001_ON_State_Rest(1–3).edf, PD_HRV_P011_ON_Rest.edf: same ECG leads
      at 256 Hz, plus an HR channel) that the ON pattern did not match. The
      trial therefore lay outside the loaded recording and was skipped with
      only a console line ('window outside recording'): no earlier workbook
      has an ON sitting value for either participant. A state's registry
      entry can now be a list of patterns; both participants list their rest
      files. Nothing else falls in those files (VALIDATION 23). The preflight
      now also lists, from the EDF headers, trials logged as under 5 s
      (placeholders — e.g. the ON 5-min sitting of P-012, P-013 and P-014,
      whose times are in the IART task files), trials outside the EDF files
      and trials with part of the window unrecorded.
[D26] Holes in the ECG recording (2.5). Where the Equivital was restarted,
      two EDF files leave a gap of ~20–35 s and the samples on either side are
      joined. In the EDF headers, 11 trials have such a hole inside the window
      (5-min quiet trials: P-018 OFF sitting and standing, P-028 OFF
      standing), 14 start or end outside the recording and 20 are not
      recorded at all. RR intervals are measured in samples, so the interval
      across a hole is not a heartbeat interval (1.1–4.7 s in samples, 33–37 s
      in time); 2.4 treated it as missed beats — excluded when long enough,
      otherwise filled with inserted beats (3 in P-028 OFF standing, 1 in
      P-005 ON walking). Now an interval whose two beats are separated by a
      jump in the ECG time stamps (> RECORDING_GAP_MIN_SEC) is flagged and its
      run excluded ('recording gap' in Reconstruction_QC): nothing is
      inserted and RMSSD does not bridge it [D22]. Data_Quality gains
      Unrecorded_Seconds (seconds of the window without ECG: holes and edges)
      and Recording_Gaps; the console and the dashboard warn from 2 s. The
      'Raw' metrics still use the detected series as it is.
      RECORDING_GAP_HANDLING = 'off' restores the previous handling (the
      holes are still reported).
[D27] Per-trial ECG lead (2.5). P-015's run sheet says "Use ECG 2 channel,
      NOT ECG 1 for the 5 minutes of quiet sitting". On ECG 1 that trial loses
      about a quarter of its beats (254 detected vs 340 on ECG 2; 103 vs 3
      beats dropped in excluded stretches); its standing and walking trials
      give the same beat counts on both leads (VALIDATION 23). The registry
      can now name the lead for a trial ('ecg_channel': {'OFF_5min_Sitting':
      'ECG 2'}). load_recording then reads that lead as well; if it is not
      recorded throughout the window, the trial falls back to ECG_CHANNEL
      with a console warning. Data_Quality reports ECG_Channel and Run_Config
      the leads per trial. On ECG 2, detection is the same with and without
      INVERT_ECG (340 beats either way).

